.\" Manpage for bicfm
.TH BICFM 1 "September 2025" "BICFM" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
bicfm \- Binary Integrity & Control Flow Monitor
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B bicfm
[\fI--db /path/to/db.json\fR] init \fIdir\fR
.br
.B bicfm
[\fI--db /path/to/db.json\fR] scan \fIfile\fR
.br
.B bicfm
[\fI--db /path/to/db.json\fR] scan-dir \fIdir\fR
.br
.B bicfm
[\fI--db /path/to/db.json\fR] show \fIfile\fR
.br
.B bicfm
\fI--help\fR \fI--version\fR
.SH DESCRIPTION
bicfm is a lightweight CLI utility to build and verify a baseline for ELF binaries.
It stores a human-readable JSON baseline containing SHA256, size, section names/sizes
and basic compiler metadata. Use bicfm to detect tampering (hash mismatch), structural
anomalies (section additions/removals/size changes), or metadata changes.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B init \fIdir\fR
Scan \fIdir\fR recursively for ELF binaries and build a baseline. The baseline is
saved to the JSON file specified by \fI--db\fR (default: \fI./bicfm_db.json\fR).
.TP
.B scan \fIfile\fR
Check a single binary against the baseline. Output is color-coded:
green = OK, yellow = Suspicious, red = Violation, blue = Info.
.TP
.B scan-dir \fIdir\fR
Scan all ELF binaries under \fIdir\fR and print per-file results plus a summary at the end.
.TP
.B show \fIfile\fR
Display baseline information for the specified file (path, realpath, sha256, size, sections, compiler).
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B --db \fIpath\fR
Path to baseline JSON file. If omitted the tool uses \fI./bicfm_db.json\fR.
.TP
.B --help
Show help and usage summary.
.TP
.B --version
Show version information.
.SH EXIT STATUS
The bicfm commands return 0 on success, non-zero on error or misconfiguration.
.SH EXAMPLES
.TP
Create baseline for /bin (default DB created in current directory):
.br
.nf
$ ./bicfm.py init /bin
.fi
.TP
Scan a single binary:
.br
.nf
$ ./bicfm.py scan /bin/ls
.fi
.TP
Use a specific DB:
.br
.nf
$ ./bicfm.py --db /var/lib/bicfm/db.json init /usr/bin
$ ./bicfm.py --db /var/lib/bicfm/db.json scan /usr/bin/ls
.fi
.SH FILES
.TP
.B bicfm_db.json
Default baseline JSON file written to current directory unless \fI--db\fR is used.
.SH NOTES
- The tool is ELF-specific (Linux). It uses the ELF magic bytes to detect ELF files.
- For scanning system directories you may need to run the \fIinit\fR command with root (sudo) privileges.
.SH AUTHOR
Software Security Lab (HPRCSE Group) \- template adapted for Lab05
.SH SEE ALSO
readelf(1), sha256sum(1), objdump(1)

